// SOUNDNESS QUEUE row closure_fnonce_call_in_loop_multi_free (tier 1). AN
// `FnOnce` CLOSURE CALLED IN A LOOP DESTROYS ITS CAPTURE ONCE PER ITERATION.
//
// `while i < 3 { r = f(); i = i + 1; }` over a closure whose body moves the
// capture out runs `D::drop` THREE times on one value. Rust refuses the program
// ("use of moved value: `f`, value moved here, in previous iteration of loop").
//
// ROOT, by symbol: THERE ARE TWO MOVE ANALYSES AND ONLY ONE OF THEM WAS TAUGHT
// THE RULE. `SemaChecker::lower_call` now marks an `FnOnce` callee moved
// (`consumes_callee` -> `mark_moved`), and the straight-line second call is
// refused by the `moved_vars_.count(callee)` test at the head of its
// `is_closure || is_fn_ptr` arm — proven by fail/closure_fnonce_called_twice_fail.
// The LOOP spelling is not, because sema's per-branch save/restore reverts
// `moved_vars_` around a loop body (stated in `mark_moved`'s own comment) and
// the loop-aware refusal lives in the OTHER analysis: borrow_check.cpp's move
// record, whose diagnostic is "use of moved value '{}' (moved on line {})".
// That analysis walks the LIR and does not treat an `EClosureCall`'s callee as
// a move of the callee. MEASURED SEPARATION on this build: the same loop over a
// plain `fn eat(d: D)` IS refused with that sentence, and the closure spelling
// is not — one analysis, two answers, same program shape.
//
// ⚠ INHERITED, NOT INTRODUCED: the base binary febd1aa6e49ae30c 43 reads the
// same count 3.
//
// ⚠ THE ORACLE IS A DESTRUCTOR COUNT: today 3, exit 1; correct is a REFUSAL
// (observed would become `refuses`, i.e. the program must not compile) — the
// program is illegal Rust and the fix is the diagnostic, not an exit code.
package closure_fnonce_call_in_loop_multi_free;

struct D { v: i64, c: *mut i64 }
impl Drop for D { fn drop(self: &mut D) { unsafe { *self.c = *self.c + self.v; } } }

fn main() -> i32 {
    let mut n: i64 = 0i64;
    let p: *mut i64 = &mut n;
    let mut r: i64 = -1i64;
    {
        let x: D = D { v: 1i64, c: p };
        let f = move || -> i64 { let t: D = x; return t.v; };
        let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
        while i < 3i64 { r = f(); i = i + 1i64; }
    }
    let got: i64 = unsafe { n };
    if got != 1i64 { return 1i32; }
    return 0i32;
}
